              "text": "چهار — chahâr — four."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two syllables, and the long â of باران in the second."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart — three languages, three fates for one sound",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "چهار (chahâr) continues Old Persian chathwar, from Indo-European kʷetwóres, \"four.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That opening kʷ is the interesting part, because three languages did three different things with it. Latin kept it whole: quattuor. English lost the lip-rounding and then the k itself softened to f: four. Persian pushed it forward to a ch. One sound, three descendants, and the numbers still line up."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
